# RISoTTo — Apptainer definition.
#
# RIbonucleic acid Sequence design from TerTiary structure: generates RNA sequences
# predicted to fold into a given backbone, conditioned on the surrounding molecular context
# (proteins, other nucleic acids, ligands, ions). The architecture closely follows CARBonAra,
# the same group's protein sequence design model.
#
#   apptainer build --fakeroot --build-arg RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR=cu12 risotto-cu12.sif risotto.def
#   apptainer build --fakeroot --build-arg RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR=cu13 risotto-cu13.sif risotto.def
#
# READ THIS BEFORE PICKING cu13.
#
# Upstream pins `torch==2.3.1` in requirements.txt and tests on CUDA 12.1. That build
# compiles sm_50 through sm_90, so it covers Volta, Ampere, Ada and Hopper — but it has NO
# BLACKWELL KERNELS, and no CUDA 13 build of torch 2.3.1 exists. On an sm_100/sm_120 card it
# dies at model init with
#   CUDA error: no kernel image is available for execution on the device
#
# So the two builds are not equivalent, and the difference is not only CUDA:
#
#   cu12  Exactly upstream's pinned environment (torch 2.3.1). This is the FAITHFUL build
#         and the one to trust for results you intend to publish.
#   cu13  Upstream's requirements with the torch pin REMOVED and a current CUDA 13 torch
#         installed instead. This is the only way to use Blackwell, but it is NOT a
#         configuration upstream has tested. Torch minor releases have changed reduction
#         order and default matmul precision before now, and this model samples from a
#         probability distribution, so a changed numeric path can move the sequences it
#         produces. Spot-check against a cu12 run before relying on it: the demo in the
#         upstream README is deterministic for `seq_wt` and `seq_0`, which makes it a usable
#         check (see %help).
#
# Weights ship inside the repository (~15 MB), so there is nothing to download at run time.
#
# EternaFold is deliberately NOT built. It is a git submodule needed only to recompute the
# 2D self-consistency numbers in the paper's benchmark, not to design sequences, and it
# needs a C++ toolchain. The clone below is therefore not recursive.

BootStrap: docker
From: python:3.11.14-slim-bookworm

%arguments
    RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR=cu12

%labels
    Maintainer  Benedikt Singer
    Tool        RISoTTo
    Upstream    https://github.com/LBM-EPFL/RISoTTo
    Commit      890758882e585ea7ac2567d5801ef3d91e77a80a
    Accelerator {{ RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR }}
    License     CC BY-NC-SA 4.0

%environment
    export RISOTTO_HOME=/opt/RISoTTo
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1
    export PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1

    # Large complexes are memory-hungry: upstream measured ~8.8 GB peak for a 9,800-atom
    # structure, which only fits a 12 GB card with this allocator setting. It costs nothing
    # on a larger card, so it is on by default.
    export PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True

    # The SIF is read-only and $HOME may be unwritable or shared between concurrent jobs.
    export XDG_CACHE_HOME=/tmp/risotto-cache
    export MPLCONFIGDIR=/tmp/risotto-cache/mpl
    export CUDA_CACHE_PATH=/tmp/risotto-cache/nv
    export TORCH_HOME=/tmp/risotto-cache/torch

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    export PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1

    RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR={{ RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR }}
    RISOTTO_COMMIT=890758882e585ea7ac2567d5801ef3d91e77a80a

    apt-get update
    # gcc/g++ are here for parmed, which requirements.txt pins at 4.3.0 and which PyPI
    # publishes as an sdist only -- no wheel for any version or platform. It compiles
    # parmed.amber._rdparm, so without a C++ compiler the whole install fails late with
    # `error: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'g++'`. The toolchain is removed again
    # below, once the install is done.
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ca-certificates git gcc g++
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    python -m pip install --no-cache-dir "uv>=0.13.4"

    # Not recursive: the only submodule is EternaFold, which is benchmark-only (see header).
    git clone https://github.com/LBM-EPFL/RISoTTo.git /opt/RISoTTo
    git -C /opt/RISoTTo checkout --quiet "${RISOTTO_COMMIT}"
    rm -rf /opt/RISoTTo/.git

    cd /opt/RISoTTo

    case "${RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR}" in
        cu12)
            # Faithful: requirements.txt exactly as pinned, torch 2.3.1 from PyPI (cu121).
            uv pip install --system --no-cache -r requirements.txt
            ;;
        cu13)
            # Blackwell: same requirements with the torch pin dropped, then a CUDA 13 torch.
            # The grep is anchored so it removes the torch pin and nothing that merely
            # contains the string (torchvision, torchaudio) if upstream adds one later.
            grep -v -E '^torch==' requirements.txt > /tmp/risotto-req-notorch.txt
            uv pip install --system --no-cache --torch-backend=cu130 "torch"
            uv pip install --system --no-cache -r /tmp/risotto-req-notorch.txt
            ;;
        *)
            echo "RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR must be cu12 or cu13 (got '${RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR}')" >&2
            exit 2
            ;;
    esac

    # Drop the compiler again. autoremove is the risky part: it also takes whatever the
    # toolchain pulled in, and the PYCHECK below is deliberately placed AFTER it so a
    # removal that breaks torch fails the build here rather than someone's first job.
    apt-get purge -y gcc g++
    apt-get autoremove -y
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    # Fail the BUILD, not the first job, if the torch that landed cannot serve the
    # accelerator this image claims. cu13 must carry Blackwell kernels; cu12 must not have
    # been silently upgraded past the pin.
    # The shell variable is not visible inside the heredoc unless it is exported, and a
    # check that silently reads an empty string would assert nothing at all.
    export RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR_CHECK="${RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR}"
    python - <<'PYCHECK'
import os

import parmed
import torch

accel = os.environ.get("RISOTTO_ACCELERATOR_CHECK", "")
archs = torch.cuda.get_arch_list()
print("torch", torch.__version__, "archs", archs)
print("parmed", parmed.__version__, "(compiled extension imported OK after the purge)")

if accel == "cu13":
    assert any(a in archs for a in ("sm_100", "sm_120")), \
        "cu13 build has no Blackwell kernels: {}".format(archs)
elif accel == "cu12":
    assert torch.__version__.startswith("2.3.1"), \
        "cu12 build is meant to be upstream's pinned torch 2.3.1, got {}".format(torch.__version__)
PYCHECK

    # Convenience entry point. apply_model.py resolves its imports relative to the
    # repository root, so the wrapper changes directory rather than relying on cwd.
    printf '#!/bin/sh\ncd /opt/RISoTTo && exec python apply_model.py "$@"\n' > /usr/local/bin/risotto-design
    chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/risotto-design

    chmod -R a+rX /opt/RISoTTo
    du -sh /opt/RISoTTo

%test
    set -eu
    # CPU-only and driver-free on purpose, so it passes on a build node with no GPU.
    # IT PROVES NOTHING ABOUT THE TARGET CLUSTER: it never touches the driver and never
    # launches a CUDA kernel. Only a real job clears the driver and arch gates.
    python -c "import torch; print('torch', torch.__version__); print('archs:', torch.cuda.get_arch_list())"
    test -f /opt/RISoTTo/save/model.pt
    test -f /opt/RISoTTo/test_pdb/1csl.pdb

    # A real forward pass on the CPU, against the structure upstream ships for exactly this.
    # seq_wt and seq_0 are deterministic, so this checks the model actually runs, not just
    # that the imports resolve.
    cd /opt/RISoTTo
    python apply_model.py --pdb_filepath=test_pdb/1csl.pdb --output_dir=/tmp/risotto-selftest \
        --num_samples=1 --device=cpu --sampling=max_confidence > /dev/null
    test -s /tmp/risotto-selftest/1csl_designs.fasta
    head -2 /tmp/risotto-selftest/1csl_designs.fasta
    rm -rf /tmp/risotto-selftest
    echo "RISoTTo self-test OK"

%runscript
    exec risotto-design "$@"

%help
    RISoTTo — context-aware geometric deep learning for RNA sequence design.

    Design sequences for an RNA backbone, keeping its molecular context:

      apptainer run --nv --bind "$PWD":/data risotto-cu12.sif \
          --pdb_filepath /data/structure.pdb \
          --output_dir /data/out \
          --num_samples 5 \
          --imprint_ratio 0.5 \
          --sampling max_confidence \
          --device cuda:0

    Output is a FASTA in --output_dir. `seq_wt` is the native sequence read off the
    structure and `seq_0` the maximum-confidence design, taken from the imprint-free
    prediction before any sampling. `seq_1` onwards are sampled and differ between runs.
    Each header carries `recovery` (identity to native) and `confidence` (mean predicted
    confidence).

    HOW REPRODUCIBLE ARE THOSE? Measured on the shipped test_pdb/1csl.pdb, 28 nt:

      seq_wt   exact, every run, every device. It matches upstream's shipped
               1csl_designs.fasta byte for byte.
      seq_0    nothing random touches it, but a position sitting at a near-tie can still
               flip on a GPU: across five cuda runs, four gave upstream's sequence and one
               flipped the last nucleotide (A -> U). Two CPU runs were stable and both
               matched upstream.

    So treat a one-position difference in seq_0 as float noise rather than as a finding,
    and run comparisons on the CPU when you need an exact answer.

    The input PDB should include the context you want the design conditioned on — proteins,
    other nucleic acids, ligands and ions all count. Context is the point of the method.

    CHECKING A cu13 IMAGE AGAINST THE PINNED ONE. cu13 does not use upstream's pinned torch
    (see the header of this file), so it is worth asking whether that moved the numerics.

    RUN THE COMPARISON ON THE CPU. Both images can, and it is the only way to separate a
    torch-version effect from ordinary GPU nondeterminism -- which on this structure is
    large enough to flip a nucleotide by itself:

      for sif in risotto-cu12.sif risotto-cu13.sif; do
        apptainer run $sif --pdb_filepath /opt/RISoTTo/test_pdb/1csl.pdb \
            --output_dir /tmp/$sif.out --num_samples 0 --device cpu
      done
      diff <(grep -A1 '^>seq_0' /tmp/risotto-cu12.sif.out/1csl_designs.fasta) \
           <(grep -A1 '^>seq_0' /tmp/risotto-cu13.sif.out/1csl_designs.fasta)

    Identical output means the newer torch did not move this model's numerics on that
    structure. It is one structure, not a proof, but a difference here is a clear warning.
    Comparing on the GPU instead gives you a one-position diff with no information in it.

    ONE MORE THING ABOUT --num_samples. The sampling loop runs until it has collected
    num_samples DISTINCT sequences (`while len(seqs_set) < num_samples + 1`), discarding
    repeats. For a short or heavily constrained RNA the model may simply not produce that
    many distinct answers, and the loop has no iteration cap -- so an over-ambitious
    --num_samples does not fail, it hangs. If a job stops making progress, that is where
    to look.

    CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 — non-commercial use only.
